================================================================================
 What the two-electron screening does to zeta                        t = 50.02 s
================================================================================
   one-electron X2C against the default, which adds the atomic mean field
    shell    X2C-1e [cm^-1]    X2C-AMF [cm^-1]      change
   ------  ----------------  -----------------  ----------
       3p           2870.85            2536.65      -11.6%
       3d            168.82              84.34      -50.0%
   ------  ----------------  -----------------  ----------
   the screening always lowers a spin-orbit constant; the one-electron operator misses the shielding of the nucleus by the other electrons
   3d spin-orbit constant                                     84.34  cm^-1
   implied 2D5/2 - 2D3/2 splitting                           210.85  cm^-1   ((2l+1) zeta / 2, one electron, frozen orbitals)
   experiment (NIST)                                         168.34  cm^-1   (an anchor, not a target)
   a frozen average-of-configuration constant in a split-valence basis is
   expected above the measured splitting: the orbitals do not relax, there
   is no correlation, and a fitted parameter absorbs both.

